  <h1>Google Business Profile for architects</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    About 18,100 times a month, someone types "architect near me" and Google
    answers with a map and three names. That shortlist is assembled by an
    algorithm, costs nothing to enter, and most firms have never once opened
    the profile that decides whether they are on it.
  </p>

  <p>
    We build paid pipelines for architecture firms, so a free channel is an odd thing for us to open with. This one earns it. The map sits above the rankings your <a href="/seo-for-architects">SEO for architects</a> work is grinding toward, entry is a profile that takes an afternoon, and most of your competitors have abandoned theirs.
  </p>

  <div class="answer">
    <p class="answer-label">The short answer</p>
    <p>To get an architecture firm into Google's map pack: claim the profile, pick the most specific category, fill every field, load real project photos, and collect reviews on a schedule, asked for at each project's happiest week. Profile signals and reviews together drive roughly half the local ranking, and the bar is low: across 12 US metros in October 2026, the median firm in the map pack had about 15 Google reviews. Residential firms get leads straight off the map. Commercial firms get their due-diligence check passed, which is quieter and worth just as much.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>The shortlist Google runs without you</h2>
  <p>
    Search anything with local intent, "architect near me" or "residential
    architect" plus your city, and Google plants a map near the top of the page
    with three businesses under it. The local pack. Those three get the profile
    visits, the calls, and the quiet authority of looking like the answer.
    Position four might as well be page nine.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now the uncomfortable part. Google builds a profile for your firm whether
    you participate or not, assembled from whatever the internet happens to say
    about you. Unclaimed, that can mean a map pin pointing at the dry cleaner
    next door, hours you have never kept, and one photo of your office door
    from 2019. Buyers assume you chose all of it.
  </p>

  <h2>"But Maps is for plumbers"</h2>
  <p>
    That is the standard objection, and the numbers disagree. "Architect near me" averages about 18,100 US searches a month (Google now counts "architects near me" as the same search), and "architecture firms near me" adds another 9,900.
  </p>
  <p>
    The demand is seasonal: 14,800 a month through the summer of 2026, 27,100 in March. The same click averages $10.02 when you buy it as a Google ad, with top-of-page bids running from $3.45 to $16.06. The map hands it to you free.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>residential</strong> work, the map is a direct lead source. A homeowner types "architect near me" late in the journey, usually with land bought and a builder already in their ear, which makes them one of the most qualified strangers who will ever go looking for you. Three firms get shown. Everyone else is, functionally, closed.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>commercial</strong> work, developers do not pick architects off a map, and it would worry us if they did. Your profile works a different shift: due diligence. Search your firm's name and the profile takes over the right side of the screen. <a href="https://www.brightlocal.com/research/local-consumer-review-survey/" rel="nofollow">BrightLocal's 2026 review survey</a> puts Google at 71 percent of consumers, still the biggest place people read reviews, and a developer vetting a shortlist behaves the same way, just with a bigger spreadsheet.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is the same Google check that quietly filters your referrals, the one we covered in <a href="/referral-systems-for-architects#5-survive-the-google-check">referral systems for architects</a>: your name comes up at dinner, and before dessert someone has searched it. The profile is what they find. Empty is an answer too. And whatever the profile promises, the click lands on your website next, where <a href="/website-design-for-architects">website design for architects</a> decides whether the visit survives.
  </p>

  <h2>What decides who makes the pack</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/7091?hl=en" rel="nofollow">Google names three factors</a>: relevance, distance, and prominence. In <a href="https://whitespark.ca/local-search-ranking-factors/" rel="nofollow">Whitespark's 2026 survey of local search experts</a>, profile signals carry 32 percent of the local pack decision and reviews another 20 percent. Half the game lives inside a profile most firms opened once, at gunpoint, in 2021.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same survey added a column for the first time this year: what gets a business named inside AI answers. The experts put reviews at 16 percent of that and the profile itself at 12, with citations (your name, address and phone matching across directories) at 13.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the review habit below feeds the map and the AI answer at once. One habit, paid twice. The wider case for being the firm an AI recommends is in <a href="/ai-search-for-architects">AI search for architects</a>. The parts you control are not mysterious.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Pick the category like money depends on it</h3>
  <p>
    Your primary category is one of the heaviest signals you get to choose.
    Pick the most specific one Google offers for what you actually sell, not
    the vaguest one that felt safe. Then make the rest of the profile agree
    with it: the services list, the description, the service area. Write them
    for a buyer, not a search engine. Keyword stuffing reads as desperate to
    both.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Fill in every field Google gives you</h3>
  <p>
    Google's own guidance says complete profiles are more likely to show up in
    local results, which is about as close as Google gets to handing out an
    answer key. Address or service area, hours, description, services, real
    contact details. It is unglamorous data entry, it takes one afternoon, and
    it is the cheapest ranking work you will ever do.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Run reviews on a schedule, not on luck</h3>
  <p>
    Reviews are a fifth of the ranking and most of the persuasion, and they
    respond to systems. Ask at the peak: move-in week for residential clients,
    opening day or the first occupancy numbers for commercial ones. Then answer
    every review you get.
  </p>
  <p>
    In BrightLocal's 2026 survey of 1,002 US consumers, 49 percent trust online reviews as much as a personal recommendation, so half your buyers read them skeptically. The same survey found 44 percent care whether a review was posted in the last month and 89 percent expect the owner to reply. A considered owner reply is the part that reads as real.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Post photos of finished work</h3>
  <p>
    You sell the way buildings look, and the default photos on an unclaimed
    profile are whatever Street View saw that day. Load finished projects and
    keep loading them as work completes, because a profile that visibly moves
    reads as a firm that is busy. No stock, no renders passed off as builds.
    Buyers can tell.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Do not get clever with addresses</h3>
  <p>
    A fake office to enter a second city's pack is the local version of buying
    backlinks: it works until Google notices, then the whole profile gets
    suspended while your real leads bounce. If you work from home, set the
    profile up as a service-area business and hide the address. Eligible,
    honest, and it keeps your living room off the internet.
  </p>

  <h2>How many reviews it took to make the pack in 12 US cities</h2>
  <p>
    Advice on this topic runs on adjectives ("lots of reviews", "a strong profile"), so we counted. On 3 October 2026 we pulled the live mobile results for "architect near me" in 12 US metros, searching from each city, and recorded the three firms Google put in the map pack. The pack sat at the very top of the page in all twelve, above every organic result.
  </p>
