    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Across the 36 pack slots, the median business had 15.5 Google reviews and the middle half had between 8 and 23. Twenty-five of the 36 had fewer than 20. In Seattle, Atlanta, Boston and Phoenix a business with one or two reviews held a spot.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only four of the 36 had 50 or more, and the biggest pile anywhere was 63. The bar is lower than most firms assume, and in most metros twenty well-timed reviews would put you in the upper half of what Google is already rewarding.
  </p>
  <p>
    Volume is not the whole game, though. In five of the twelve metros the firm in first place was not the one with the most reviews, which is distance and relevance doing their share.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ratings barely separate anyone: 35 of the 36 sat at 4.5 stars or higher. And in Atlanta and Boston a professional association held a pack spot (AIA Atlanta, and the Boston Society for Architecture's BSA Space), while another Atlanta spot went to a landscape design company. Those are slots a firm with a filled-in profile could take.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things to read into it by segment. If you do <strong>residential</strong> work, the pack is winnable on habit, and the profiles in it mostly belong to small studios, not big names.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>commercial</strong> work, note who else showed up: HKS in Dallas with 63 reviews, Gensler in Houston, Goettsch Partners in Chicago. Large commercial practices keep their profiles reviewed, which tells you their clients look. The demand side of the same metros, searches per city and what a click costs in each, is in <a href="/architecture-client-demand-by-metro-2026">architecture client demand by metro</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The limits, stated plainly: this is one snapshot on one day, from a searcher placed in each city center, so a buyer in the suburbs would see a different three. Packs move week to week. Treat the counts as the order of magnitude, not a target to hit exactly.
  </p>
  <p>
    The next thing a buyer does after the map is tap through to the website. We opened the homepages of the firms in these packs the following day, and what they show a stranger (7 of 28 said who they build for, 1 said anything about cost) is in <a href="/branding-for-architects#what-28-firms-in-googles-map-pack-show-a-buyer-in-the-first">branding for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the map stops working</h2>
  <p>
    The pack rewards proximity, not portfolio. Distance is one of Google's
    three factors, and no amount of optimizing moves your office. You compete
    inside a radius Google decides, for whoever happens to search this week,
    ranked by an algorithm that offers you no dials. You cannot show up to more
    buyers than exist, and you cannot choose which ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the gap between capturing demand and creating it. The profile catches people already looking for an architect nearby. It does nothing about the buyers who have not started searching, homeowners quietly planning a build if you do residential, developers and business owners with a project taking shape if you do commercial. Reaching them first, at a volume you control, is the job of paid ads, and the full case is in <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects">Meta ads for architects</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    So run both, with expectations in the right order: the profile is free capture, set up once and maintained lightly, and the paid engine is where growth actually gets decided. How the two slot into one system is mapped in <a href="/architecture-marketing">architecture marketing</a> and built out in <a href="/lead-generation-for-architects">lead generation for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>A few honest answers</h2>
  <h3>Can you pay Google for a better local ranking?</h3>
  <p>
    Not the organic pack. Google's words: "There's no way to request or pay for a better local ranking on Google." What money can buy is the sponsored slot that sometimes sits above the map, which is a different game with different math, covered in <a href="/google-ads-for-architects#what-you-are-actually-buying">Google Ads for architects</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>How many reviews do I need?</h3>
  <p>
    Fewer than you think. In our 12-metro count the median map-pack firm had 15.5 Google reviews and 25 of the 36 had fewer than 20, so the realistic target is the count of the three firms Google shows in your city today, plus fresher.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recency matters to both sides: 44 percent of consumers in BrightLocal's 2026 survey care whether a review was posted in the last month. Two reviews a quarter, asked for at the happiest moment of each project, can quietly pass firms twice your age.
  </p>
  <h3>I do commercial work across three metros. Does any of this matter?</h3>
  <p>
    The pack matters least for you. The profile still matters. You will not
    map-pack in a city where you have no real address, and faking one gets the
    profile suspended.
  </p>
  <p>
    But every developer who hears your name runs the brand
    search, and a profile with reviews from past clients is what answers it.
    Treat it as your due diligence page, not your lead source, and let the
    outbound engine do the traveling. Press coverage works the same shift, which is <a href="/pr-and-press-for-architects">PR and press for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Claim the map this week</h2>
  <p>
    An afternoon of setup, a photo habit, a review ask built into project
    closeout, and you are ahead of most firms in your metro on a channel that
    costs nothing but attention. Then build the part no map gives you: demand
    you can turn up on purpose, from buyers who were never going to search.
    Building that engine for architecture firms is what we do all day.
  </p>
